# build output
/agent-bus
/agent-busctl
/bin/
*.test

# runtime data — the durable store is never committed
/data/
*.wal
*.log
!AGENT_LOG.md

# local agent config / scratch
/.claude/settings.local.json
/scratch/

# scripts/gen-spec-mirror.sh builds the whole SPEC/ tree here and swaps it in by
# rename, so the staging dir MUST be a sibling of SPEC/ (same filesystem) rather
# than under /tmp. It is removed on exit, including on failure; this pattern
# exists so a crashed run cannot leave a half-built mirror looking like tracked
# content. SPEC.md and SPEC/ themselves ARE tracked — they are the generated
# mirror and the server-down fallback.
/.spec-tree.*

# secrets — NEVER commit key material (CORE-10)
#
# This is a mechanical backstop, not a policy. Anything that stages with
# `git add -A` (a stop hook, a `git commit -a`, an agent tidying up) will
# happily commit a private key that landed in the working tree, because none of
# its guards look for credentials. These patterns make that a non-event.
#
# It becomes materially more important once the CRYPTO epic starts writing
# agent Ed25519 private keys and the bus's self-signed TLS key to disk
# (invariants 3 and 11) — at that point key material in the tree is the normal
# case rather than an accident.
#
# The real spec-cloud credentials already live OUTSIDE the repo and must stay
# there; this is defence for the material we cannot keep out.
#
# NOT listed, deliberately: *.crt / *.cer. A certificate is the PUBLIC half —
# invariant 11 pins the bus's self-signed cert fingerprint into the invite blob
# precisely so it can be handed around — and a test fixture certificate is a
# thing this repo may legitimately want to track. Ignoring the public half buys
# no secrecy and would silently drop a fixture someone meant to commit.
#
# If you ever need to commit one of the files below on purpose, `git add -f`
# says so explicitly and shows up in review. That is the intended escape hatch.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
.env
.env.*
*credentials*
*-creds*
*_creds*
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.token
#
# The agent-busctl CREDENTIAL STORE (CLI-2-FU-GITIGNORE). Checked, not assumed:
# none of the patterns above matches `identities.json` — it is not a `.pem`, not
# a `.key`, and the substring rules want "credentials"/"creds", not "identities".
# It nevertheless holds TWO Ed25519 private-key SEEDS per enrolled agent
# (`private_key_seed` and `messaging_key_seed`), which makes it the single
# highest-value file this repo can accidentally acquire.
#
# It normally lives outside the tree — `DefaultIdentityDir` is
# `os.UserConfigDir()/agent-bus` — so this is for the case that puts it here:
# `--identity ./somewhere` or `AGENT_BUS_IDENTITY=$PWD/...`, which is exactly
# what a throwaway run or a copy-pasted example does.
#
# The `.tmp-*` companion is NOT belt-and-braces. `(*Store).saveJSON` writes to
# `<name>.tmp-<random hex>` and renames, and `sweepTempFiles` only clears
# leftovers on the NEXT write — so a process killed mid-save leaves a COMPLETE
# COPY OF EVERY PRIVATE KEY under a name the bare pattern does not match.
identities.json
identities.json.tmp-*
#
# cursors.json is ignored TOO, and it is deliberately not credential material —
# so the reasoning is stated rather than waved through. Three parts:
#   - It is not innocuous. It is a map of (agent id -> bus id -> read position):
#     precisely the routing metadata invariant 6 keeps in the audit trail, and
#     committing it publishes which agents talk to which buses.
#   - The two files are written by the SAME code into the SAME 0700 directory
#     and arrive together, so catching one and not the other shrinks no blast
#     radius — the seeds are already covered — while guaranteeing a permanently
#     dirty tree in the one situation where a clean `git status --porcelain` is
#     the signal work is finished (CLAUDE.md step 10).
#   - Its temp file leaks nothing, but it is listed for the same reason: a rule
#     that covers a file but not its rename source is a rule with a hole in it.
# Cost, stated honestly and it is the same cost `*.pem` already accepts: a
# credential store dropped into the tree now contains only ignored files, so the
# directory stops showing up in `git status` and nothing shouts about it. `git
# add -f` remains the deliberate, review-visible escape hatch.
cursors.json
cursors.json.tmp-*

# ...but NEVER a source or script file, and never a directory. The three name
# patterns above are unanchored substring matches, so without these negations a
# perfectly ordinary `internal/auth/credentials.go`, `session_creds.go`,
# `credentials_test.go`, `scripts/rotate-credentials.sh` or `credentials.md`
# would be silently ignored — and an ignored file leaves `git status
# --porcelain` CLEAN, which is precisely how half a change gets left behind in
# the working tree (CLAUDE.md step 10; it has already left `main`
# un-compilable in this repo once).
#
# Negation only re-includes paths an earlier pattern excluded, so these lines
# cannot widen what is tracked beyond that over-match. They do NOT rescue
# `*.pem` / `*.key` / `*.token` — nothing named `foo.key` is source.
#
# The directory negations are separate and necessary: those patterns match
# DIRECTORIES too, and git will not even descend into an excluded directory, so
# a negation on a file inside one cannot fire. Without them an
# `internal/credentials/` package would be invisible in its entirety. Cost,
# stated honestly: a data file inside such a directory (`credentials/api.json`)
# becomes trackable again.
!*credentials*/
!*-creds*/
!*_creds*/
#
# ...and the same negation for the two KEY-NAME patterns above, which the
# 2026-08-08 review missed because nothing generated directory names then.
# `id_rsa*` and `id_ed25519*` are unanchored PREFIX patterns and they match
# DIRECTORIES too. `scripts/gen-spec-mirror.sh` now generates one directory per
# backlog task, named from the task's title — so a task called "id_ed25519 seed
# import" produces `SPEC/CRYPTO/id_ed25519-seed-import--<id>/`, git declines to
# descend into it, `!*.md` below never gets the chance to fire, and the task
# VANISHES from the mirror while `git status --porcelain` stays perfectly clean.
# That is the silent-drop failure this repo keeps legislating against, arriving
# through .gitignore instead of through code. Reproduced in a throwaway repo by
# the security gate (2026-08-14); the generator now also asks git directly, every
# run, whether it is ignoring any file the generator just wrote.
#
# Cost, stated honestly and it is exactly the cost `!*credentials*/` already
# accepts above: an extension-less FILE inside a directory whose name starts
# with `id_rsa`/`id_ed25519` (`id_rsa.d/agent01`) becomes trackable again. A key
# stored at such a path is NOT protected by these patterns — only keys named
# `id_rsa*` themselves, and `*.pem`/`*.key`/`*.token`, still are. `git add -f`
# remains the deliberate, review-visible escape hatch in the other direction.
!id_rsa*/
!id_ed25519*/
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT NEGATED — reviewed 2026-08-08 and both gates agreed:
#   *.yml / *.yaml — YAML is a DATA format, not source. `credentials.yml`,
#     `db_creds.yaml`, `prod-creds.yaml` are far likelier to BE credentials than
#     to be code about them, and negating them gave back the highest-value slice
#     of this control for no benefit (the only YAML in this repo is
#     `docker-compose.yml`, which no pattern above matches anyway).
#   .env.example / *.json.example — a documented real-world leak vector: the
#     file exists to be copied and someone eventually fills in a live value.
#     Neither exists here. If one ships, `git add -f` it deliberately.
!*.go
!*.sh
!*.md
!*.py
#
# ...and `.env.*` back LAST, because the last matching pattern wins. A dotenv
# file is never source whatever suffix it carries, and `!*.sh` above would
# otherwise rescue `.env.sh` — a very common way to spell "a file full of
# exported secrets".
.env.*
